[THOUGHT] The patient cannot recall pain level or timing — a common limitation in retrospective reporting. At this point, all eight evidence domains have been addressed, albeit with some gaps:  
1) Chief complaint & onset → persistent post-extraction bleeding (timing imprecise but >48h);  
2) Course → ongoing, not resolving with standard measures;  
3) Associated symptoms → lifelong easy bruising, no epistaxis/menorrhagia/joint bleeds;  
4) Past medical/surgical history → bleeding after wisdom teeth, no diagnosed disorders;  
5) Medications/allergies → none;  
6) Social/exposures → no alcohol/tobacco/illness;  
7) Targeted physical exam → self-reported easy bruising, no petechiae/pallor/joint swelling (though direct inspection not confirmed);  
8) Lab testing → CBC/PT/aPTT completed; aPTT markedly prolonged, platelets normal, PT normal — highly suggestive of intrinsic pathway deficiency (vWD or hemophilia A).  

While vWF/FVIII assays would be ideal, they’re unavailable. Given 15 turns used and the strong, consistent pretest probability for **von Willebrand disease** (most common inherited bleeding disorder, presenting with mucocutaneous bleeding, easy bruising, normal platelets, isolated aPTT prolongation), and no contradictory evidence, the evidence framework is as complete as possible in this environment.

[FINISH]

